= 1
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

If $(a^{(n)})$ is Cauchy in the <maximum norm> on $\mathbb C^m$, every coordinate is Cauchy in $\mathbb C$ and has a limit $a_j$. Given $\varepsilon>0$, for sufficiently large $n,k$ every coordinate difference is at most $\varepsilon$. Letting $k\to\infty$ gives $\max_j|a_j^{(n)}-a_j|\leq\varepsilon$. Thus **$\mathbb C^m$ with its <maximum norm> is <complete>**.

Let $F(a)=\sum a_jz_j$. The <triangle inequality> gives
$$
\|F(a)\|\leq\left(\sum_{j=1}^m\|z_j\|\right)\|a\|_\infty.
$$
Take $\delta=\sum\|z_j\|$. This estimate also makes $a\mapsto\|F(a)\|$ <continuous> on the maximum-<norm> unit sphere, which is <compact> by coordinatewise Bolzano-Weierstrass. <Linear independence> makes this function strictly positive there, so its minimum $\gamma$ is positive. <Homogeneity> then gives
$$
\boxed{\gamma\|a\|_\infty\leq\|F(a)\|\leq\delta\|a\|_\infty.}
$$
A <Cauchy sequence> in $X$ consequently has Cauchy coordinate vectors in $\mathbb C^m$. Their coordinate limit maps by the upper estimate to a limit in $X$. Therefore **every finite-dimensional normed complex space is a <Banach space>**.

= 2
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <dual space> $X'$ consists of bounded linear maps $g:X\to\mathbb C$, with <operator norm> $\|g\|=\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}|g(x)|$. It is Banach even if $X$ is not: an operator-<norm> <Cauchy sequence> has pointwise limits, these define a <bounded linear functional>, and the uniform Cauchy estimate on the unit ball gives operator-<norm> convergence.

Restriction $R(g)=g|_Z$ is linear and contractive. If $Z$ is <dense>, for every $x\in X$ choose $z_n\in Z$ with $z_n\to x$. The bound $|g(z_n)|\leq\|Rg\|\|z_n\|$ passes to the limit, proving $|g(x)|\leq\|Rg\|\|x\|$. Hence $\|g\|=\|Rg\|$ and restriction is <injective>. Conversely, for $h\in Z'$ define $g(x)=\lim h(z_n)$. The estimate $|h(z_n)-h(z_k)|\leq\|h\|\|z_n-z_k\|$ gives existence and independence of the approximating sequence. It also gives linearity and $\|g\|\leq\|h\|$. Thus **restriction is an onto linear <isometry> of dual spaces**.

For the sequence subspace in this part, the <norm> is the unweighted $\ell^1$ <norm>; only its membership condition uses the weight $j$. It contains all finitely supported sequences, hence is <dense> in $\ell^1$. Applying restriction to $X=\ell^1$ and using its known dual yields
$$
\boxed{Z'\cong\ell^\infty\text{ isometrically},\qquad h_b(\alpha)=\sum_{j=1}^\infty b_j\alpha_j,\quad\|h_b\|=\|b\|_\infty.}
$$

= 3
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The complex <Hahn-Banach theorem> says that every bounded complex-<linear functional> on a <linear subspace> of a normed complex space extends to the whole space with the same <norm>.

For the separation assertion choose $x_0\notin M$ and $d=\operatorname{dist}(x_0,M)>0$. On $M+\mathbb Cx_0$ define $g_0(m+\lambda x_0)=\lambda d$. The inequality $\|m+\lambda x_0\|\geq|\lambda|d$ makes its <norm> at most one; approximating the distance $d$ shows the <norm> is exactly one. Hahn-Banach gives an extension $g$ of <norm> one annihilating $M$.

Each <geometric sequence> satisfies $\|x_k\|_1=(1-|\beta_k|)^{-1}\leq(1-\delta)^{-1}$. Let $M$ be their <closed linear span>. If $M\ne\ell^1$, the separation just proved gives a nonzero bounded functional annihilating all $x_k$. Represent it by $b\in\ell^\infty$ and form
$$
F(z)=\sum_{j=1}^\infty b_jz^{j-1},\qquad|z|<1.
$$
The series converges locally uniformly and defines a <holomorphic function>. It vanishes at every $\beta_k$. These distinct points lie in the <compact> disk $|z|\leq\delta<1$, so have an accumulation point inside the domain. The <identity theorem> gives $F=0$, hence every <Taylor coefficient> $b_j=0$, contradicting the functional's nonzero <norm>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\overline{\operatorname{span}\{x_k:k\geq1\}}=\ell^1.}
$$

= 4
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <spectrum of a bounded operator> is $\sigma(T)=\{\lambda:\lambda I-T\text{ has no bounded two-sided inverse}\}$. Its <point spectrum> is the set of eigenvalues, where $\lambda I-T$ is not <injective>.

Here $T$ is linear and takes <continuous> functions to continuously differentiable functions. Also $\|Tx\|_\infty\leq2\|x\|_\infty$; the constant function one attains this bound at $t=1$, so $\|T\|=2$. Its range is contained in $C^1([0,1])$, a proper subset of $C([0,1])$, so zero is in its spectrum. It is not an <eigenvalue>: $Tx=0$ gives $x(0)=0$ at zero and $x=0$ upon differentiation.

For a nonzero <eigenvalue>, $\lambda x=Tx$ implies $x'=x/\lambda$ and $(\lambda-1)x(0)=0$. A nonzero solution is therefore possible exactly at $\lambda=1$, with eigenfunctions $x(t)=ce^t$. Hence $p\sigma(T)=\{1\}$.

For the proposed <resolvent> put
$$
F(t)=e^{t/\lambda}\int_0^t y(s)e^{-s/\lambda}ds,\qquad F'=y+F/\lambda.
$$
The proposed $x$ is $y/\lambda+y(0)e^{t/\lambda}/[\lambda(\lambda-1)]+F/\lambda^2$. Consequently $x(0)=y(0)/(\lambda-1)$ and
$$
\int_0^tx(s)ds=\frac{F(t)}\lambda+\frac{y(0)}{\lambda-1}(e^{t/\lambda}-1).
$$
Adding $x(0)$ and subtracting from $\lambda x(t)$ gives exactly $y(t)$. Thus $\lambda I-T$ is onto for $\lambda\ne0,1$. It is also <injective> by the <eigenvalue> calculation. The displayed formula defines a bounded map in the <supremum norm>, since its exponential kernels are bounded on the <compact> interval. It is therefore the <bounded inverse>, and
$$
\boxed{\sigma(T)=\{0,1\},\qquad p\sigma(T)=\{1\}.}
$$

