= Solution
{parent=5}

Use the convention that the <inner product> is linear in its first argument. If $v\ne0$, nonnegativity of $\|u-\langle u,v\rangle v/\|v\|^2\|^2$ gives
$$
\boxed{|\langle u,v\rangle|\leq\|u\|\|v\|.}
$$
The case $v=0$ is immediate. This <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> shows that $f_v$ is bounded linear with <norm> at most $\|v\|$. For $v\ne0$, evaluation at $v/\|v\|$ gives equality; for $v=0$ both norms vanish. The Banach dual is the space of all bounded linear functionals with the <operator norm>.

The <Riesz representation theorem> asserts that every $f\in V'$ is uniquely $f(u)=\langle u,v\rangle$ for some $v\in V$, with $\|f\|=\|v\|$. If $f=0$, take $v=0$. Otherwise put $W=\ker f$, a <closed subspace>, and use the projection theorem to obtain $w\in W^\perp$ with $f(w)=1$. Since $u-f(u)w\in W$, $\langle u,w\rangle=f(u)\|w\|^2$. Thus $v=w/\|w\|^2$ represents $f$. If two vectors represent it, their difference has <inner product> zero with every vector, in particular itself, giving uniqueness. The <norm> statement was already proved.

The space $P_n$ is finite-dimensional and hence <complete>; evaluation at zero is bounded there by equivalence of finite-dimensional norms. Riesz therefore supplies exactly one $q_n\in P_n$. In any <orthonormal> <polynomial> basis,
$$
\boxed{q_n(t)=\sum_{j=0}^n\overline{p_j(0)}p_j(t),\qquad\|q_n\|^2=\sum_{j=0}^n|p_j(0)|^2.}
$$
For the real Legendre functions the bars may be omitted.

**The printed even-degree normalization has an error.** <Orthogonality> forces a degree-two <polynomial> to be a scalar multiple of $3t^2-1$, whose squared integral on $[-1,1]$ is $8/5$. Hence any <orthonormal> choice has $|p_2(0)|^2=5/8$, whereas the printed formula gives $3/8$. The factor under its square root must be $4m+1$ instead of $2m+1$. Signs of the individual polynomials can be chosen so that the values at zero are positive; these signs do not affect $q_n$ or its <norm>.

Put $M=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ and $c_m=\binom{2m}{m}/4^m$. With the consistent <orthonormal> normalization the <norm> is
$$
\boxed{\|q_n\|^2=\frac12\sum_{m=0}^M(4m+1)c_m^2=\frac{(2M+1)^2c_M^2}{2},\qquad\|q_n\|=\frac{(2M+1)c_M}{\sqrt2}.}
$$
The finite sum telescopes because $c_m=(2m-1)c_{m-1}/(2m)$, so $(2m+1)^2c_m^2-(2m-1)^2c_{m-1}^2=(4m+1)c_m^2$. If one substitutes the erroneous printed values formally instead, one obtains $\frac12\sum_{m=0}^M(2m+1)c_m^2$; that is not the <norm> of the actual <orthonormal> Legendre evaluation representer.

For a <polynomial> $r$ of degree $d$, the defining identity gives $\langle r,q_n\rangle=r(0)$ for every $n\geq d$, so the requested limit is eventually constant. It does **not** extend to all of $L^2$. First, evaluation at a point is not well-defined on equivalence classes. More strongly, the functional norms $\|q_n\|$ are unbounded. Indeed the largest binomial coefficient in row $2m$ is at least the average, so $c_m\geq1/(2m+1)$, and the displayed sum dominates a divergent constant multiple of the <harmonic series>. The <uniform boundedness principle> then provides an $L^2$ function for which $\sup_n|\langle r,q_n\rangle|=\infty$. Thus no choice of point representative can restore the assertion for all $L^2$ functions.

= 6
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Write $L_n(t)=\sum_{k=0}^na_kt^k$. Matching coefficients in the differential equation gives
$$
(k+1)^2a_{k+1}+(n-k)a_k=0.
$$
Taking $a_0=1$ yields
$$
\boxed{L_n(t)=\sum_{k=0}^n(-1)^k\binom nk\frac{t^k}{k!}.}
$$
The top coefficient is $(-1)^n/n!\ne0$, so this is a real <polynomial> of degree exactly $n$.

Put $D_n=L_n'-L_n$. <Integration by parts>, with vanishing endpoint terms because of the factor $t$ at zero and exponential decay at infinity, gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_0^\infty tD_nD_me^{-t}dt
&=-\int_0^\infty[(tD_ne^{-t})'+tD_ne^{-t}]L_mdt\\
&=-\int_0^\infty[D_n+tD_n']L_me^{-t}dt\\
&=(n+1)\int_0^\infty L_nL_me^{-t}dt.
\end{aligned}
$$
The last step uses $D_n+tD_n'=tL_n''+(1-t)L_n'-L_n=-(n+1)L_n$. The left side is symmetric in $m,n$. Interchanging them therefore gives $(n-m)\int L_nL_me^{-t}=0$, proving <orthogonality> for distinct indices. These integrals are exactly $\langle\varphi_n,\varphi_m\rangle$, and each $\varphi_n$ is square-integrable and nonzero. Thus they form an <orthogonal> sequence in $L^2(0,\infty)$.

Multiplying the equations by $z^n$ and summing gives
$$
\boxed{tG_{tt}+(1-t)G_t+zG_z=0.}
$$
Conversely equality of power-series coefficients recovers every <polynomial> equation. For the proposed expression,
$$
\frac{G_t}G=-\frac z{1-z},\qquad\frac{G_{tt}}G=\frac{z^2}{(1-z)^2},\qquad\frac{G_z}G=\frac1{1-z}-\frac t{(1-z)^2}.
$$
Substitution makes the PDE left side zero. Its coefficient of $z^n$ has value one at $t=0$, so it corresponds to the normalization $L_n(0)=1$ chosen above.

