= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\lambda=a+ib$ with $b\ne0$. Since $aI-T$ is self-adjoint, $\langle(aI-T)v,v\rangle$ is real. The cross term in the squared <norm> therefore vanishes:
$$
\|(\lambda I-T)v\|^2=\|(aI-T)v\|^2+b^2\|v\|^2=\|(\overline\lambda I-T)v\|^2.
$$
This proves the equality and lower bound, and makes both $S=\lambda I-T$ and $S^*=\overline\lambda I-T$ <injective>.

If $Sv_n$ converges, the lower bound shows $v_n$ is Cauchy. <Completeness> gives $v_n\to v$ and boundedness gives $Sv_n\to Sv$, so the range is closed. Also $(\operatorname{ran}S)^\perp=\ker S^*=0$, so the range is <dense>. Being both closed and <dense>, it equals $V$. Hence $S$ is bijective, and its lower bound gives
$$
\boxed{\|(\lambda I-T)^{-1}\|\leq|\operatorname{Im}\lambda|^{-1},\qquad\sigma(T)\subseteq\mathbb R.}
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Define $f^\sharp(s)=\overline{f(-s)}$. Interchanging the integrals and substituting variables gives
$$
\langle T_fg_1,g_2\rangle=\langle g_1,T_{f^\sharp}g_2\rangle.
$$
Moreover Cauchy-Schwarz in the inner integral gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
|\langle T_fg_1,g_2\rangle|
&\leq\int_{\mathbb R}|f(s)|\left|\int_{\mathbb R}g_1(t-s)\overline{g_2(t)}dt\right|ds\\
&\leq\|f\|_1\|g_1\|_2\|g_2\|_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
Taking the supremum over unit $g_2$ proves $\|T_fg_1\|_2\leq\|f\|_1\|g_1\|_2$, so $T_f$ is bounded with <norm> at most $\|f\|_1$, and the adjoint identity is justified.

It remains to ensure necessity is a condition on the <kernel> itself. If $T_h=0$ for some $h\in L^1$, convolve $h$ with a smooth compactly supported <approximate identity> $\eta_\varepsilon\in L^1\cap L^2$. Since $T_h\eta_\varepsilon=0$, all these convolutions vanish. But $h*\eta_\varepsilon\to h$ in $L^1$, by continuity of translations in $L^1$, so $h=0$ almost everywhere. Thus the map from kernels to <convolution> operators is <injective>. Applying this to $h=f-f^\sharp$ yields the exact criterion
$$
\boxed{T_f=T_f^*\iff f(s)=\overline{f(-s)}\text{ for almost every }s.}
$$
For real-valued kernels this reduces to evenness; real-valuedness by itself is insufficient.
